  "novel_hypothesis_tested": "L9 (the finale) combines the whole series: a serpentine MAZE occupying the south half with the thief spawned at its far end, a fence-socket row, a solid wall with a single row-8 gap, three bays (one large, two small), nine blocks, and two role-specialized helpers. Key findings: (1) the maze graph is a linear corridor chain whose ONLY exit to the playfield is the 1-wide corridor c3 mouth at tile (10,3) -- the thief needs ~44 ticks to serpentine out, a huge free-work window; (2) the NE helper is a FENCE-EXTRACTION SPECIALIST: it idles until a block is socketed into the row-3 fence, then extracts it from the north side and delivers it to the NE bay -- the fence is a hand-off channel across an otherwise impassable row (discovered by an accidental socket in episode 1, exploited deliberately thereafter); (3) the post-maze thief BEELINES THE BIG BAY, not loose blocks; the reliable kill is intercepting its first bay-raid grab-tick -- landed at tick 48, mid-grab, before the block ever left its slot; (4) the win fired on the ninth simultaneous placement with GameState.WIN at 9/9 levels.",
  "narrative": "Three-episode arc. Episode 1 (recon): executed an east-side triple-ferry opening (two direct bay placements plus an accidental fence-socket that revealed the NE helpers extraction role), measured the thiefs maze-exit timing, and lost the endgame to its bay raids at 7-placed. Episode 2: refined opening (three ferries including a deliberate socket-relay by tick 29), raced the thief to the maze mouth and lost the cork by one tick, killed nothing, ended 6-placed with the hostage dumped harmlessly. Episode 3 (winning): byte-identical opening through tick 29, then instead of contesting the mouth, shadowed the thiefs known bay-beeline and killed it AT ITS FIRST GRAB-TICK (tick 48) -- the stolen block never left its slot; with zero opposition the west helper and I split the last two ferries and the ninth placement landed at tick ~66 with four timer cells to spare: levels_completed 9/9, GameState.WIN -- the full game cleared. The candidate sequence is prefix + the 66-action winning episode.",
  "l9_gotchas": [
    "The maze is a linear corridor chain with ONE exit (tile (10,3)); the thief takes ~44 ticks to emerge -- treat that as a free-work window, and note the cork-race at the mouth is a 1-tick coin flip, so prefer the bay-grab intercept.",
    "The post-maze thief ignores loose blocks and BEELINES the big bay: its first grab-tick is deterministic and interceptable -- the highest-probability kill of the level.",
    "The NE helper only extracts fence-socketed blocks (north-side relay to the NE bay); socket into the row-3 fence to activate it -- direct deliveries to the NE bay from the south are geometrically impossible for the player.",
    "The west helper self-runs the entire west cluster at ~7 ticks/delivery; leave the west blocks to it.",
    "L9 timer drains ~0.83 cells/action (~77-action episodes): the level is a strict time-budget puzzle; the winning allocation was 30 ticks east-ferrying, 18 getting into kill position, 18 final ferries.",
    "Fungible bays hold: any block in any bay slot counts toward the 9."
  ],
